This file is a placeholder, not real Firebase config. It uses the
".PLACEHOLDER" extension specifically so the google-services Gradle plugin
does NOT try to parse it as JSON and does NOT fail the build by its mere
presence - Gradle only looks for a file literally named "google-services.json".

To build and run this app for real:

  1. Create a Firebase project at https://console.firebase.google.com
     (any Google account works; use your university or personal account).
  2. In that project, add an Android app with package name:
         com.msc.distractionalert
     (must match applicationId / namespace in app/build.gradle exactly).
  3. Download the google-services.json file Firebase generates for that app.
  4. Place it at:
         app/google-services.json
     (i.e. delete or ignore this placeholder - the real file goes in the same
     folder, app/, next to build.gradle).
  5. Re-sync Gradle in Android Studio.

Full walkthrough: see FIREBASE_SETUP.md in the project root.

Until step 3-4 are done, the project will fail to build, because the
google-services plugin (applied in app/build.gradle) requires a real
app/google-services.json to be present.
